Using law of conservation of linear momentum
$\begin{aligned}
& u m=m v_1+2 m v_2 \Rightarrow u=v_1+2 v_2 \qquad....\mathrm{(i)} \\
& e=\frac{2}{5}=\frac{v_2-v_1}{u-0} \Rightarrow u=2.5 v_2-2.5 v_1 \qquad....\mathrm{(ii)} 
\end{aligned}$
From eq (i) & (ii) $0.5 v_2=3.5 v_1$ or, $\frac{v_1}{v_2}=\frac{0.5}{3.5}=\frac{1}{7}$

The mass of two blocks are $\mathrm{m}_1=2 \mathrm{~kg}$ and $\mathrm{m}_2=1 \mathrm{~kg}$. The acceleration of masses is given as

\mathrm{a}=\left(\frac{\mathrm{m}_2-\mathrm{m}_1}{\mathrm{~m}_2+\mathrm{m}_1}\right) \mathrm{g}=\left(\frac{2-1}{2+1}\right) \mathrm{g}=\frac{\mathrm{g}}{3}

The acceleration of the centre of mass is given by

\mathrm{a}_{\mathrm{cm}}=\left(\frac{\mathrm{m}_2-\mathrm{m}_1}{\mathrm{~m}_2+\mathrm{m}_1}\right) \mathrm{a}=\left(\frac{2-1}{2+1}\right) \times \frac{\mathrm{g}}{3}=\frac{\mathrm{g}}{9}

The distance travelled by their centre of mass is

\begin{aligned}
& \mathrm{S}=\frac{1}{2} \mathrm{a}_{\mathrm{cm}} \mathrm{t}^2 \\
& =\frac{1}{2} \times \frac{\mathrm{g}}{9} \times 2^2=\frac{2}{9} \mathrm{~g}=\frac{2}{9} \times 10 \\
& =2.22 \mathrm{~m}
\end{aligned}

Given, $m_A=50 \mathrm{gm}, u_A=10 \mathrm{~m} / \mathrm{s}, m_B=10 \mathrm{gm}u_B=-15 \mathrm{~m} / \mathrm{s}$ and coefficient of restitution, $e=\frac{2}{5}$ The collision of ball $A$ and $B$ is shown as below,

